If you read back, though, that's not the argument I'm making.

I said that nobody has _successfully demonstrated_ that the protection run
is a more effective, reliable, etc. alternative, yada yada.

There is a significant difference between the arguments.  One is the
flawed correlation-proves-causation argument, which is of course flawed.
The second, however, leans more towards the prospect of measurable 
experimentation on the subject -- and _that_ sort of thing tends to produce
results that one can rely on to make statements such as "better".

In my case (though I already regret having posted that list): I was able
to achieve 9 out of 10 ascensions, each with a separate role, through my
described methods and starting from pettype:none.  OK.  That's one
demonstration, in a known, consistent environment.

A corresponding demonstration of how the protection racket/run works would
certainly go a long way -- say, 9 out of 10 ascensions with a primary
early-game goal being "achieve naked AC 1 before, say, reaching the
Sokoban level", all with a different role.  But I haven't seen anyone
even _try_ to do this.

While it is entirely possible that nobody tries to do this just because
they haven't thought of it yet, is it also possible that we don't see
results like this because people are trying, failing, and abandoning the
idea?  

